---
description: Make interactive fur the fundamental DuckPlan button material with complete hover, press, release, and focus feedback.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Three-Stage Button Interaction Feedback

- Interactive duck-down fur is the fundamental visual material for DuckPlan buttons and button-like controls. New conventional controls should use the shared fur renderer by default instead of introducing another painted button surface.
- Text labels, icon-only labels, and icon-plus-text labels must all be formed by the same fur-strand lettering/mask system. Do not leave an icon painted or stationary on top of a fur button.
- Preserve the native semantic label and accessible name beneath the decorative fur canvas. Icon-only controls need an explicit `aria-label`.
- Repeated content controls such as calendar cells, time slots, task rows, and event blocks may remain lightweight non-fur surfaces when mounting individual WebGL contexts would harm performance or legibility. They still follow every interaction rule below.
- Mount fur renderers only for the currently visible interaction surface. Release renderers in hidden views, closed drawers, and inert page regions.
- Every enabled button and button-like control must provide three visibly distinct interaction stages, regardless of its material or visual style:
  1. **Hover:** visible while a hover-capable pointer is over the control.
  2. **Press:** immediate feedback while pointer, touch, Space, or Enter is held down.
  3. **Release:** a brief, visible response after the press is released; this must be more than simply removing the pressed style.
- Match the effect to the material. Standard controls may lift, compress, tint, or rebound; fur controls must use localized brushing/compression and visible strand recovery rather than moving the whole control.
- Interaction effects must not shift surrounding layout or reduce label readability.
- Keep a clear `:focus-visible` state for keyboard users; hover must never be the only interaction cue.
- Disabled controls must show none of the three interaction effects and must look unavailable.
- Keyboard activation must receive equivalent press and release feedback.
- Under reduced motion, replace movement with an immediate tint, outline, brightness, or elevation change while preserving all three states.
- Verify hover, held press, post-release, keyboard, disabled, and reduced-motion behavior whenever a button is added or visually changed.
